+
+## Local Index
+
+Each kiosk builds its own index from the snapshot it imports; the signed snapshot is the only input and no index is distributed. The index holds one row per record keyed by the normalized serial number, with the snapshot date and schema version stored alongside it. Lookup is a single keyed read against the active index, opened read-only, with no network access. The staging index is built and verified (row count equals the snapshot's record count) before activation. The previously active index is deleted only after the new one is active.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index, built on each kiosk at import | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Lookup must complete from files on the kiosk with no runtime network access. SQLite gives predictable keyed lookups with no service to run. Accepts that each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index on every snapshot import. |
+| Activation | Build in a staging file, then atomically switch the active index | Import in place within a transaction on the active index | A failed or interrupted build cannot affect the active index, so the last valid snapshot stays available. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. Behavior when no snapshot has ever been imported, and when the active index file is unreadable or fails its integrity check.
+2. Free-disk-space handling while the staging index and active index coexist, at snapshots up to 150 MB.
+3. Whether uniqueness is checked on the normalized serial number or the raw serial number.
